## Audience

You are an engineer who needs to:

- (a) **understand** <system-name> at an architecture level (so you can
  debug, audit, or extend); OR
- (b) **port/fork** <system-name> into a sibling project; OR
- (c) **integrate** with <system-name> as a black box.

Pick your reader profile at the start — it changes the milestone
weights at the end of this document.

## Pre-requisites

Concrete skills/knowledge the reader MUST already have. Be honest —
additions to this list raise the ramp cost; removals save time.

| Domain | Required | Nice to have |
|---|---|---|
| **Languages** | <e.g. Python 3.11+>, <e.g. TypeScript 5> | <e.g. Rust, Go> |
| **Frameworks** | <e.g. FastAPI>, <e.g. React> | <e.g. Next.js> |
| **Concepts** | <e.g. event-driven I/O>, <e.g. pub/sub> | <e.g. CRDT, Raft> |
| **Tooling** | <e.g. git, ripgrep, jq> | <e.g. mitmproxy, Wireshark> |
| **Domain** | <e.g. distributed systems fundamentals> | <e.g. payments compliance> |

## Recommended reading sequence

The order respects dependencies — don't skip chapters unless you know
exactly why. Each chapter cites paths inside this dissect (relative
to `dissects/<system>/`).

### Phase A — Orient (1-2 hours)

1. `triagem.md` — what is this system, what does it do, key
   metadata.
2. `handoff/README.md` — entry point + index of Phase 5 artifacts.
3. `handoff/components-priority.md` — what matters (Tier 1) vs
   what's cargo cult (Tier 3).
4. `deep-dive/architecture.md` — high-level architecture diagram.

**Milestone A**: You can describe the system in 3 sentences and
name its Tier 1 components from memory.

### Phase B — Understand internals (3-6 hours, paraleliza por módulo)

5. For each Tier 1 component:
   - The corresponding `deep-dive/<component>-analysis.md`.
   - The relevant `wiki/modules.md` section.
   - The corresponding `extract/components.md` row.
6. `deep-dive/data-flows.md` — how data moves between modules.

**Milestone B**: You can trace a single user request end-to-end
through the system and name the components it touches.

### Phase C — Patterns and decisions (2-4 hours)

7. `handoff/patterns-catalog.md` — the 5+ patterns you MUST
   understand before changing anything.
8. `handoff/decisions.md` — at minimum read every ADR titled with
   an inviolable constraint (look for `Good:` / `Bad:` ratios).
9. `extract/patterns.md` + `extract/algorithms.md` — long-form
   corroboration.

**Milestone C**: You can describe 3 patterns and 3 decisions
without referring to your notes.

### Phase D — Reuse assessment (1-2 hours)

10. `handoff/reuse-checklist.md` — for each Tier 1/2 component,
    mark `reuse / wrap / clean-room / skip`. Justify with C1-C6.
11. `handoff/components-priority.md` — confirm your ranked picks
    against the proposed roadmap (sequence only, not destinations).
12. (OPT-IN) `<downstream>-integrate/` if a destination-specific plan
    exists. Otherwise just consume this `handoff/`.

**Milestone D**: You can produce a written justification of one
reuse decision in 5 minutes (with citations).

### Phase E — Implementation (variable; only for reader profile (b)/(c))

13. `handoff/implementation-guide.md` — first Tier 1 component
    recipe.
14. Iterative work — see §"Exercises" below.

## Comprehension milestones — quick check

Use these as a self-test before claiming you've "learned" the system.

| Milestone | What you can do without notes |
|---|---|
| After Phase A | Describe system + Top-5 Tier 1 + tech stack anchor |
| After Phase B | Trace a user request through 5+ components |
| After Phase C | Reproduce 3 patterns + 3 decisions from memory |
| After Phase D | Defend a reuse-decision for one Tier 1 component |
| After Phase E | Implement one Tier 1 component clean-room from spec |

## Exercises (suggested)

Pick the ones that match your reader profile. They reference paths
inside this dissect (relative to `dissects/<system>/`).

### Exercise 1 (orientation, 30 min)

Open `handoff/README.md` and reconstruct the entry point without
reading anything else. Time yourself. If you can't, your
onboarding doc has gaps.

### Exercise 2 (deep dive, 1-2 h)

Pick the top Tier 1 component from `handoff/components-priority.md`.
Read:
- `deep-dive/<component>-analysis.md`
- The relevant section of `wiki/modules.md`
- The corresponding row in `extract/components.md`

Write a 1-page summary citing at least 5 `file:line` references into
the source code under `repo/` (if source-available dissect) or
`bin/` (if binary).

### Exercise 3 (pattern identification, 1 h)

Open 3 different files in `repo/`. For each, identify which pattern
from `handoff/patterns-catalog.md` it instantiates. If a file
instantiates none of the catalogued patterns, propose a new
candidate.

### Exercise 4 (decision audit, 1-2 h)

Pick one ADR from `handoff/decisions.md`. Open the source file
referenced in the YAML `source:` field. Verify the decision is
still honored by the current code. If broken, that's a regression
in the upstream system — log it in `risk-raw.md`.

### Exercise 5 (clean-room implementation, 1 day)

Pick one Tier 1 component from `handoff/implementation-guide.md`.
Implement it from scratch following only the spec — do not read
the source code under `repo/`. Compare your implementation to the
original side-by-side and write a 1-page diff analysis. This is
the Compaq/IBM "Time A / Time B" methodology (1981-83) that proved
copyright doesn't protect functional interfaces.

